{"connection_id": "BC-8d804897f4c859a3f4a7", "ref": "1 Clement 24:5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "seed decay as resurrection", "note": "The commentary connects the plant's life, death, and reappearance from earth with resurrection; Clement reads a seed buried and decaying in soil as evidence of resurrection, making the botanical sequence an explicit Christian proof-image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2242443eb352f7ad48e", "ref": "1 Clement 24:5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "seed decaying in earth and rising to fruit", "note": "The commentary’s living pasture and dead ground become a resurrection bridge; 1 Clement develops the sown seed’s decay in earth and providential rise into much fruit as evidence for resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4951e5c0de02fa951beb", "ref": "Augustine, City of God 21.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "bodies punished eternally without annihilation", "note": "The commentary’s great-fire figure is neither normally dead nor alive; Augustine argues that bodies of the damned can suffer eternal fire without being consumed, a later Christian account of that paradoxical endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f747cf55f82d15d14c81", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seed dies, then lives", "note": "The commentary moves from dead soil through living grass to decay and also discusses life and death; Paul uses a seed that must die before it comes to life to explain resurrection, making the plant cycle an explicit model for bodily renewal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4095d000a7d493d33a6", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "what is sown does not come alive unless it dies", "note": "The commentary refuses to collapse plant life into the person in fire but sets them in one frame; Paul makes the seed’s dying-and-coming-alive sequence an argument for resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-352ddec07b9433915a0c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gives each seed its body", "note": "The commentary says God brings pasture out of earth and compares the plant's life and death with resurrection; Paul says God gives each seed its own body, connecting divine creation to the form that emerges from sowing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b3c1afe1fae2bc34e52", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishable sown, imperishable raised", "note": "The commentary moves from plant decay to 87:17's more-lasting hereafter; Paul describes the resurrection body as sown perishable and raised imperishable, making permanence the direct outcome of the death-and-growth analogy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-776d31a09cafb5f72794", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sown perishable, raised imperishable", "note": "The commentary moves from perishable grass to “better and more enduring”; Paul’s resurrection contrast says what is sown perishable is raised imperishable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e4aa854d5c364624f8e3", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "planting, watering, God’s growth", "note": "The commentary supplies rain as the agency behind pasture’s emergence; Paul distinguishes his planting and Apollos’ watering from God’s giving the growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b22339ec7ffc05ec582", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God alone giving growth", "note": "The commentary calls the rain-grounded growth God’s act rather than a self-contained natural cycle; Paul’s conclusion likewise makes neither planter nor waterer decisive, but God who makes things grow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad0afc9bf98584bd3c3e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "hidden things of darkness brought to light", "note": "The commentary combines God’s knowledge of hidden things with faint light in cloud; Paul says the Lord will bring to light what is hidden in darkness and disclose the heart’s purposes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-238c94255b0dbce3233c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "world passing, God’s doer remaining", "note": "The commentary’s moving debris figures the passing world before the lasting good; this verse says the world and its desire pass away while the one doing God’s will remains forever."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ddacb9333a3565af03a8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "born again from imperishable seed", "note": "The commentary connects the plant's cycle to what is more lasting; Peter says believers are born again from imperishable seed through God's living word, adding regeneration as the durable counterpart to perishable growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc5e086d99cc05ed9161", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass and fading human glory", "note": "The commentary follows pasture from green growth to dry residue; Peter quotes the grass-and-flower image to describe human glory as perishable, carrying the plant's short life into a Christian account of mortality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a62a0b5f00f3385fd1d6", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "all flesh as grass that withers", "note": "The commentary extends pasture’s life-cycle toward the human condition; Peter directly applies the grass-and-flower image to all flesh and says the grass withers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2587a88f60a7d07003cb", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah's enduring word applied to the gospel", "note": "The commentary joins 87:5's fading plant to 87:17's more-lasting hereafter; Peter follows the grass image with the word that endures and identifies it with the proclaimed good news, giving Isaiah's contrast a Christian interpretive application."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfbff8fd89655f47f15f", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "enduring word after fading grass", "note": "The commentary asks what remains after earthly vegetation and life have passed; Peter contrasts the withering grass with the Lord’s word that remains forever."}
{"connection_id": "BC-afd4c3afb4bca0b773d7", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "temporary and eternal", "note": "The commentary contrasts the plant's brief worldly cycle with what is more lasting in 87:17; Paul contrasts what is seen and temporary with what is unseen and eternal, supplying a Christian formulation of that temporal divide."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b664bd1e698fa2d21cf", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "seen temporary, unseen eternal", "note": "The commentary makes the pasture’s vanishing a bridge to S87:17’s enduring hereafter; Paul explicitly calls visible things temporary and unseen things eternal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9432d0bde557f95f2061", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sowing reaps corruption or eternal life", "note": "The commentary contrasts the pasture's short worldly life with the better, more lasting hereafter; Paul says sowing to flesh reaps corruption while sowing to the Spirit reaps eternal life, turning field work into an ethical choice of destinies."}
{"connection_id": "BC-338a3cabb820687da142", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sowing to corruption or eternal life", "note": "The commentary’s pasture becomes a moral contrast between passing life and what endures; Paul’s sowing image makes flesh reap corruption and Spirit reap eternal life."}
