{"connection_id": "BC-a2fd4ee56375c1088f47", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "land drinking rain and producing a crop", "note": "The commentary turns rain into the cause of a pasture’s emergence; Hebrews describes land drinking repeated rain and bearing a useful crop, with divine blessing on that fertility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccf45651eed47373d470", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "thorns end in burning", "note": "The commentary traces plant life into dry residue and then places a person in fire; Hebrews says thorn-bearing land is rejected and ends in burning, joining failed growth and fire in an ethical warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dbabb9a66a9c19624f49", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "rain-fed land yielding thorns, ending in burning", "note": "The commentary follows grass from rain to blackened refuse; Hebrews deliberately reverses the fruitful field, where land that drinks rain but produces thorns is rejected and ends in burning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-33d8a8d6de162769cd06", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers under heat", "note": "The commentary makes pasture's drying a figure for life's short course; James describes scorching heat withering grass and its flower, then compares that loss to a rich person's fading life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59750f9cf97d57ffbee9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "scorching heat, grass withering, flower falling", "note": "The commentary observes both green fullness and its darkened, dried end; James gives the sequence of sun’s scorching heat, grass withering, flower falling, and beauty perishing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff7c2b2953c72aba4043", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farmer awaits early and late rain", "note": "The commentary reconstructs rain as what brings pasture from earth and notes the first rain's seasonal mark; James tells farmers to wait patiently for precious fruit through the early and late rains, adding the human labor and timing of the crop cycle."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d7f79f192aff2b841e2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "farmer, precious fruit, early and latter rain", "note": "The commentary’s first rain begins the plant’s whole life; James uses a farmer waiting for earth’s precious fruit through the early and latter rains as the ethical analogue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fe2c77195afbd218b741", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.38", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Rabbi used for teacher", "note": "The commentary treats 87:1's رب as “Lord” and develops its Arabic family separately; John records the Semitic address Rabbi and explains it as teacher, showing a cognate title in an interpersonal teaching context."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0f3e502ace563c8f930", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "entering and finding pasture", "note": "The commentary notes that المرعى names grass, the place, and grazing; Jesus says those who enter through him will be saved and find pasture, giving the pastoral word a Christian image of access and nourishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85549dec7d94f7071adf", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.10.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "entering and finding pasture", "note": "The commentary hears pasture as both site and act of grazing; Jesus says that the one entering through him will go in and out and find pasture, preserving that concrete pastoral sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d65436650898be617c46", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.11.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "life through resurrection despite death", "note": "The commentary presents a fire in which one neither dies nor lives; Jesus identifies himself as resurrection and life and says the believer will live even if he dies, an explicit opposing resolution of death and life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1fde1f30bb7f0813f772", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.12.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grain dies and bears fruit", "note": "The commentary says the plant's journey includes both life and death; Jesus compares his own death and resulting many lives to a grain falling into earth and dying, giving the botanical sequence a distinct christological reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4b0c8b8bc6ea7729d4f8", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.12.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "grain dying in earth and bearing much fruit", "note": "The commentary’s pasture emerges from soil and its life/death vocabulary opens onto human destiny; Jesus makes a grain falling into earth and dying the condition of much fruit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a1aabbc959a17079eee", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dry branches burned", "note": "The commentary's grass dries after greenness and its next image concerns fire; Jesus says branches outside the vine wither, are gathered, and are burned, linking plant desiccation to judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-45cc9b7cbe08178d91be", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "branch not abiding, withering, gathered for fire", "note": "The commentary contrasts the pasture’s fleeting life with what remains and then turns to fire; Jesus says a branch that does not abide is thrown out, withers, is gathered, and burned."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7cb58fd9c5ef6678a1c5", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water becoming a spring of eternal life", "note": "The commentary calls rain life because it brings life to earth; Jesus recasts given water as a spring welling up to eternal life, shifting the image to a person’s lasting life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-762486ac82a888f3d167", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.29", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "resurrection to life and resurrection to judgment", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the vegetative life-cycle from an ultimate human fate; Jesus speaks of those in tombs coming out either to resurrection of life or resurrection of judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-315a8cf0b5f5d8afc4a2", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "perishing food and food abiding to eternal life", "note": "The commentary contrasts worldly life with what is better and more lasting; Jesus distinguishes food that perishes from food that endures to eternal life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5c9fa1ee4d771435cb1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.54", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cloud rising and a coming shower", "note": "The commentary dwells on the cloud’s first appearance as a sign that rain will come; Jesus describes observers seeing a cloud rise in the west and correctly expecting a shower."}
{"connection_id": "BC-04604ccd9dab4d4489c7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.55", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "south wind and expected heat", "note": "The commentary gives the south wind a secondary role beside the cloud; Jesus likewise uses the south wind as a recognized weather sign, here for scorching heat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a4f87542a72f1c9c18e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "conscious speech and thirst in postmortem flame", "note": "The commentary’s fire-state is described as neither living nor dying; in Jesus’ rich-man story the dead man speaks from flame and asks for water, an explicitly conscious alternative picture of fiery postmortem punishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6ff958e28e897214a2e5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "chaff and unquenchable fire", "note": "The commentary’s grass becomes dry debris and its later ayat reach the great fire; John’s harvest image gathers chaff and burns it with unquenchable fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a412f5d8b81e519b0301", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "the hidden made manifest", "note": "The commentary’s hiddenness root is heard in weak lightning and rain drawing animals from their holes; Jesus’ saying that nothing hidden remains undisclosed provides the same movement from concealment into appearing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d607bee860adae25199", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.4.28", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stages of crop growth", "note": "The commentary compresses a plant's entire life into emergence and dry residue; Mark's seed grows through blade, head, and ripe grain before harvest, expanding the same field process into successive stages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-098e2831917e32f418a9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.4.28", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth producing grain by itself", "note": "The commentary treats ahrāja as real extraction of vegetation from soil; Jesus’ seed parable says the earth produces first stalk, then head, then full grain, making the staged emergence itself the figure."}

