{"connection_id": "BC-ddce3c4c14ff5efe63b4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.4.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sun-scorched, rootless growth withering", "note": "The commentary follows fresh green into dried refuse; the parable’s quickly sprouted plant is scorched by the sun and withers because it has no root."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dad71a54693341796880", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.9.48", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "worm not dying and fire not quenched", "note": "The commentary’s wording at S87:13 makes unending fire a state outside normal dying and living; Jesus cites Isaiah’s paired image of the worm that does not die and fire that is not quenched."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d0f2232c1c4f80318f28", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.40", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "weeds gathered and burned at the end of the age", "note": "The commentary notes that the Qur’an can transfer vegetative debris to human communities and then reaches the great fire; Jesus’ interpretation transfers weeds into people gathered for burning at the age’s end."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7eb337ba3c33f38bfb6b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.42", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "fiery furnace and conscious grief", "note": "The commentary’s fire-state is a human fate after the plant image; Jesus’ sequel says the evildoers are thrown into a fiery furnace, where there is weeping and gnashing of teeth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c3ae1a61c5fc1b41b9c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "eternal punishment and eternal life", "note": "The commentary’s “neither dies nor lives” in great fire is set against a better enduring hereafter; Jesus’ final judgment explicitly divides people between eternal punishment and eternal life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1dba1538750ee571917", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.3.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chaff burned with fire", "note": "The commentary explains غثاء as dried plant matter swept together and also describes the person in fire; John the Baptist's image separates grain from chaff and burns the chaff in unquenchable fire, bringing crop residue into judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e9116365e68df1def9ec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.30", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "living grass thrown into an oven", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the plant's ordinary green-to-dry cycle from the person who neither lives nor dies in fire; Jesus says field grass is alive today and tomorrow goes into the oven, sharpening that contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3dfc0e2a7cc59b035cec", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.30", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "field grass today, fire tomorrow", "note": "The commentary’s pasture is briefly green before its terminal transformation, while S87 later speaks of great fire; Jesus calls field grass present today and cast into an oven tomorrow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01ad872c57111b29b102", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "hallowed name in heaven", "note": "The commentary makes praise of the divine name the entry into its heaven-to-earth image; Jesus’ prayer begins by hallowing the Father’s name and locating the addressee in the heavens."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb1a0ed50376f5aafa68", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "high exaltation and name above every name", "note": "The commentary’s first command joins the divine name to highestness; Paul says God highly exalted Jesus and granted him the name above every name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f78a3a65da24edce8ecb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "torment without rest", "note": "The commentary describes a person remaining in fire without death or life; Revelation portrays the condemned as having no rest day or night, a related image of unending suffering with a different emphasis."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3be556ddd4752f1eb5a6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "smoke rising forever and no rest", "note": "The commentary’s fire leaves no ordinary death or life; Revelation describes the smoke of torment rising forever and its worshippers having no rest day or night."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6df9ee189c4c9503b0e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "lake of fire called second death", "note": "The commentary stresses that the one in the fire neither dies nor lives; Revelation names the lake of fire “the second death,” a direct difference in how the fiery afterlife is framed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87ea29ea35e1600b8a33", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "lake of fire called the second death", "note": "The commentary calls the fire-state neither death nor life; Revelation instead names the lake of fire “the second death,” a close but deliberately different way of construing final punishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f16d7a520dc893dbe439", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "river of water of life", "note": "The commentary ends with the hereafter as better and more lasting; Revelation's final vision has a river of life flowing from God's throne, placing life-giving water in the renewed world beyond the plant cycle."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e3d7f82ede9640fef69", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree of life bears fruit continually", "note": "The commentary contrasts the pasture's decay with the hereafter's permanence; Revelation's final city has a tree of life bearing fruit each month with healing leaves, an enduring counter-image to seasonal loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-474e055492cff3d209a5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "tree of life with unceasing fruit and healing leaves", "note": "The commentary sets darkened, dried pasture beside the Qur’anic image of enduring lush gardens; Revelation’s final garden has the tree of life yielding fruit each month and leaves for healing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-43f8d7c467f5a8634642", "ref": "1 Enoch 51:1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth and Sheol returning their deposits", "note": "The commentary’s extracted pasture becomes a model for “bringing out” the dead; 1 Enoch says the earth and Sheol will give back what was entrusted to them."}
