{"connection_id": "BC-b6df9ee189c4c9503b0e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "lake of fire called second death", "note": "The commentary stresses that the one in the fire neither dies nor lives; Revelation names the lake of fire “the second death,” a direct difference in how the fiery afterlife is framed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87ea29ea35e1600b8a33", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "lake of fire called the second death", "note": "The commentary calls the fire-state neither death nor life; Revelation instead names the lake of fire “the second death,” a close but deliberately different way of construing final punishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f16d7a520dc893dbe439", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "river of water of life", "note": "The commentary ends with the hereafter as better and more lasting; Revelation's final vision has a river of life flowing from God's throne, placing life-giving water in the renewed world beyond the plant cycle."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e3d7f82ede9640fef69", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree of life bears fruit continually", "note": "The commentary contrasts the pasture's decay with the hereafter's permanence; Revelation's final city has a tree of life bearing fruit each month with healing leaves, an enduring counter-image to seasonal loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-474e055492cff3d209a5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "tree of life with unceasing fruit and healing leaves", "note": "The commentary sets darkened, dried pasture beside the Qur’anic image of enduring lush gardens; Revelation’s final garden has the tree of life yielding fruit each month and leaves for healing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-43f8d7c467f5a8634642", "ref": "1 Enoch 51:1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth and Sheol returning their deposits", "note": "The commentary’s extracted pasture becomes a model for “bringing out” the dead; 1 Enoch says the earth and Sheol will give back what was entrusted to them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7986996d49003e225231", "ref": "2 Baruch 50:2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth restoring what it received", "note": "The commentary’s ground moves from lifelessness to living growth and then resurrection; 2 Baruch says the earth will surely restore the dead it now receives."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ffc5c7735c561e5823d", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "King of the world raising to everlasting life", "note": "The commentary’s final scale is a better, enduring afterlife beyond mortal destruction; the second brother expects the King of the world to raise him to an everlasting renewal of life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bdfffd8ef5047b250f66", "ref": "4 Ezra 7:32", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth giving back the dead", "note": "The commentary’s “bringing out” of life from earth is extended to the dead; 4 Ezra says the earth will give back those asleep in it and the dust those dwelling silently there."}
{"connection_id": "BC-91df08e3ad92d1469bc2", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "this world and the world to come compared", "note": "The commentary reads the pasture’s passing cycle into S87:16–17’s world-to-come contrast; Avot weighs a moment of repentance and good deeds here against a moment of bliss in the world to come, preserving the tension rather than simply equating the two."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b14937909d026d75bf13", "ref": "Mishnah Ta'anit 1:1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "seasonal mention of the power of rain", "note": "The commentary names the first rain and treats rain as a bridge toward the life/death horizon; the Mishnah debates when the “power of rain” begins to be mentioned in communal seasonal prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-686dec1f14e8dee945cc", "ref": "Qaddish, opening formula", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sanctification and magnification of the Name", "note": "The commentary opens with glorifying the Lord’s name; the Jewish doxology asks that God’s great name be magnified and sanctified, offering a liturgical counterpart to that act."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-c56cfeb65c066ae41f7d", "ref": "Rashi on Genesis 2:5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "rain awaited for vegetation", "note": "The commentary treats rain as the unnamed link between pasture emerging and its later decay; Rashi reads Genesis' pre-rain plants as waiting for Adam to pray, after which rain falls and vegetation grows."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4cf9756e44cf93300d44", "ref": "Sirach 14:18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "leaves falling and new leaves growing", "note": "The commentary makes the complete plant life-cycle interpret human mortality; Sirach compares generations of flesh and blood to a tree whose leaves fall while others grow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e36b13a9fe0d2764a433", "ref": "Ta'anit 2a", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain and resurrection", "note": "The commentary treats rain as the source of earthly life and links plant life and death to resurrection imagery; the Talmud places the divine keys of rain and resurrection together, making their shared dependence on God's power explicit without equating them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4980c2f485ec7529184", "ref": "Targum Jonathan on Isaiah 66:24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "corpses read as sinners’ souls in Gehenna", "note": "The commentary’s question is how a person can remain in fire without ordinary death or life; the Targum’s rendering of Isaiah shifts the visible corpses to sinners’ souls and explicitly situates their punishment in Gehenna."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7ed5e8519d47c238ef9", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.44", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "first cloud before rain", "note": "The commentary describes the cloud's first appearance before rain brings life; Elijah's servant sees a small cloud rising from the sea as the first sign of rain after drought, a concrete cloud-to-rain scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b910701e39b816b2c94", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.44", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "first small cloud rising", "note": "The commentary treats hurūj as the cloud’s first appearance; Elijah’s servant sees a cloud only “like a man’s hand” rising from the sea, a sharply drawn first emergence before the rain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c57fe3c060f5014a8374", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.45", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud, wind, downpour", "note": "The commentary links a persistent cloud and wind with the rain that brings pasture; this verse describes the sky darkening with cloud and wind before heavy rain, presenting the same weather sequence in Elijah's drought story."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6bb60f38553c76ddce8c", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.45", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "black sky, cloud, wind, heavy rain", "note": "The commentary joins a lingering cloud and the south wind to the coming rain; the sequel makes the sky black with clouds and wind before a great rain, adding a narrated weather sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0506dce1d37ce1a00172", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.23.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "clear light after rain, grass from earth", "note": "The commentary’s first green follows rain after a cloud-and-lightning scene; David’s last words compare righteous rule to clear morning light after rain that makes grass spring from earth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-517e8084f5fe01791ee0", "ref": "WLC:Amos.4.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "rain on one plot, another withering", "note": "The commentary notes first green appearing patchily, “in one place and not another”; Amos describes rain sent on one field while the field without rain withers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b620701576a41c4ec89", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sleepers in dust awaken", "note": "The commentary sets plant death and life beside the person who neither dies nor lives and the lasting hereafter; Daniel describes sleepers in the dust awakening to everlasting life or disgrace, making final destinies explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bdea4d7eb35e4c8ba92b", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "dust sleepers to everlasting life or abhorrence", "note": "The commentary’s final contrast includes durable afterlife and the terrible non-life of fire; Daniel’s dust sleepers awaken either to everlasting life or to everlasting contempt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50f3e655b15858ed1222", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God knows hidden things and light dwells with him", "note": "The commentary reads S87:7’s divine knowledge of what is open and hidden beside cloud-light; Daniel says God reveals deep and hidden things and that light dwells with him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d28368959f84a3cc765", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.35", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "world-empires like chaff carried by wind", "note": "The commentary notes that the Qur’an can make human communities, not only grass, into flood-debris; Daniel makes shattered kingdoms into chaff carried off by the wind."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0321a536a5e405d0946d", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.44", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "kingdom that will not be destroyed", "note": "The commentary balances grass’s disappearance with the better, lasting hereafter; Daniel contrasts the wind-carried debris of prior kingdoms with God’s kingdom that will never be destroyed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-844457ad47bca7c0b3d6", "ref": "WLC:Deut.11.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "early and latter rain for the land", "note": "The commentary calls the first rain vesmī and makes seasonal rain the source of vegetation; Deuteronomy promises early and latter rain so the land’s grain, wine, and oil can be gathered."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73fb6b1c1db24ef0d280", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses, rain, grass", "note": "The commentary closes by recalling Moses and links divine guidance with pasture; Moses' song pictures teaching as rain and words as dew falling on grass, joining revelation to the scene's weather and growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06496fa122ed625cb57f", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teaching falling as rain", "note": "The commentary connects rain-made emergence to the surah’s later reminder and names Moses at its close; Moses asks that his teaching fall like rain and his speech distil like dew on grass."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28a9639e108f84aac523", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "proclaiming YHWH’s name", "note": "The commentary begins with the imperative to glorify the Lord’s name; in the immediately following line Moses says he will proclaim YHWH’s name and calls hearers to ascribe greatness to God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc04938f871a65164c18", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.39", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God kills and makes alive", "note": "The commentary relates the plant's life and death to 87:13's person who neither dies nor lives, and recalls Moses at the surah's close; Moses' song says God kills and makes alive, making divine control of those states explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d625eaab5f4786a811a", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "full clouds emptying rain on earth", "note": "The commentary infers rain as the causal middle between cloud and pasture; Qoheleth states the connection plainly: clouds full of rain empty it on the earth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40ec9afc748931ca7aa5", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "watching wind and clouds instead of sowing and reaping", "note": "The commentary imagines someone spending the night watching lightning to learn where it will flash; Qoheleth turns weather-observation into an agricultural warning, since one who watches wind will not sow and one who looks at clouds will not reap."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f650c3c16c4b45b959f1", "ref": "WLC:Exod.13.21", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud guides Israel, fire lights night", "note": "The commentary develops cloud, weak lightning, and a Moses connection; Exodus says God leads Israel by a cloud in daytime and fire at night, adding a guidance scene where cloud and light mark the divine route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5efd7c00c55c7fb8a8fe", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.17.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "east wind making a growing vine wither", "note": "The commentary assigns wind a role in the cloud-and-grass sequence and ends with dried vegetation; Ezekiel’s vine is flourishing until an east wind strikes it and makes it wither where it grew."}
{"connection_id": "BC-639f1cd37d55357d9a03", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.17.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "green tree dried and dry tree made to flourish", "note": "The commentary’s creator both brings out pasture and turns it into dark refuse; Ezekiel makes the reversal explicit when God dries the green tree and makes the dry tree flourish."}

