{"connection_id": "BC-b47399b76b42bffade70", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חיה, live", "note": "The commentary labels يَحْيَىٰ with Arabic root ح ي ي and calls rain ḥayā because it gives earth life; Ezekiel uses cognate Hebrew חיה when God promises life to dry bones, shifting the shared life verb from vegetation to bodily restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ef8ed438caca184d0f2", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "breath entering dry bones so they live", "note": "The commentary moves from dead ground to living vegetation and then to S87:13’s life/death problem; Ezekiel’s vision has God promise dry bones breath so that they will live."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f2dd333e1722775393c", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ي ي source label and Hebrew חיה", "note": "The source commentary explicitly labels S87:13’s “live” with Arabic Ḥ-Y-Y; Ezekiel’s חיה, “you shall live,” is its clear Hebrew cognate and applies life to dry, death-marked matter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1987f3e0d1fa848c9ac9", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.47.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruitful trees beside life-giving water", "note": "The commentary contrasts the pasture's decay with what lasts; Ezekiel's restored-land vision has trees beside a river whose fruit continues and whose leaves heal, a sustained botanical image in an eschatological setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2fcd358e4fe85f5a3e2", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.47.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "leaves not withering beside life-giving water", "note": "The commentary places the darkening pasture beside an imagined undrying plant; Ezekiel’s river trees bear continual fruit and their leaves do not wither because their water flows from the sanctuary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4f7ed6bb15100fbf733", "ref": "WLC:Gen.1.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earth brings forth vegetation", "note": "The commentary describes 87:4 as God concretely bringing pasture out; Genesis has God command the earth to bring forth vegetation and seed-bearing plants, adding the creation scene in which the ground yields plant life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b2521005fa90068708b7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.1.30", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "green vegetation as food for living creatures", "note": "The commentary says المرعى contains grass, its place, and grazing; Genesis gives every green plant to the animals as food, supplying the feeding relation embedded in pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71632b17580efc38c411", "ref": "WLC:Gen.14.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew עליון, Most High", "note": "The commentary renders 87:1's الأعلى as “Most High”; Genesis uses the cognate Semitic title El Elyon for the creator of heaven and earth, anchoring the shared elevation term in a blessing of God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba3551b0ba2eb192c9dd", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no rain before plants", "note": "The commentary stresses that rain is absent from 87:4 even as it supplies the missing link; Genesis says shrubs had not yet appeared because there was no rain and no person to work the ground, making the absent conditions explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d20a68afb309bfdb4de", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "no rain, no field plant, no cultivation", "note": "The commentary deliberately infers the unnamed rain needed for pasture; Genesis explicitly says that no field shrub or plant had yet grown because YHWH had not sent rain and there was no human to cultivate the ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59285d289fed7a7943d2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mist waters the ground", "note": "The commentary reconstructs rain behind pasture emerging but notes that 87:4 does not name it; the next Genesis verse instead has water rise from earth and water its surface, offering a distinct mechanism for the ground's fertility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-336c1ad6e6cedb044010", "ref": "WLC:Hos.10.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fallow ground broken for righteousness", "note": "The commentary calls unseeded, unrepaired soil “dead land”; Hosea turns breaking fallow ground, sowing, and reaping into an ethical summons to seek YHWH."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21a8e34e378153f3529c", "ref": "WLC:Hos.13.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "morning mist, dew, wind-driven chaff, smoke", "note": "The commentary moves from cloud and first moisture to plant-debris, then notes that such debris can figure people; Hosea describes the guilty as morning mist, vanishing dew, whirlwind-driven chaff, and smoke."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8614f47a22e409cfd061", "ref": "WLC:Hos.14.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dew makes Israel blossom", "note": "The commentary links rain to the life of earth and pasture; Hosea says God will be like dew to Israel, which will blossom like a lily, using moisture-fed growth to portray national restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5872f7e9ff694607daa0", "ref": "WLC:Hos.6.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "revival and seasonal rain", "note": "The commentary calls rain life for earth and later treats the plant's life and death alongside the hereafter; Hosea promises Israel revival and likens the Lord's coming to seasonal rains that water the land, joining renewal to rain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f08726437e8417cf33a", "ref": "WLC:Isa.25.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic م و ت source label and Hebrew מות", "note": "The source commentary labels S87:13’s “die” with Arabic M-W-T; Isaiah uses the cognate Hebrew מות when God swallows death forever, turning the shared death-word toward eschatological victory."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8bc8dd5608afe384cef0", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.19", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "dead living and dust-dwellers awakening", "note": "The commentary links ground-made life to the later horizon of death and life; Isaiah commands those dwelling in dust to awake because the dead will live and corpses rise."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-0782939d427143880565", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.23", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "rain for seed and broad pasture for cattle", "note": "The commentary joins rain, ground-emergence, and the feeding sense of pasture; Isaiah promises rain for sown seed, rich produce, and cattle grazing in a broad pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-32701673a346aa2f0d5d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.4.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cloud by day, flaming brightness by night", "note": "The commentary’s overhead shade and cloud-edge light are joined in Isaiah’s Zion vision, where a cloud and smoke appear by day and flaming brightness by night over the whole site."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca1fce56b80cbb64fc74", "ref": "WLC:Isa.4.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "shade and refuge from storm and rain", "note": "The commentary glosses samāʾ as what rises above and casts shade; Isaiah calls the canopy a shade by day and a refuge from storm and rain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13cdd1a48f86b4bc21cf", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers", "note": "The commentary follows pasture from green life to dry debris; Isaiah says grass withers and the field flower fades, then uses that plant cycle to describe mortal human glory."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e84f5ab8545f963bd96", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "grass withering under the divine breath", "note": "The commentary develops fresh green becoming dry and blackened; Isaiah says grass withers and flower fades when YHWH’s breath blows on it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2dcdb3b3e60360426ee", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "what endures", "note": "The commentary connects the pasture's brief cycle to 87:17's claim that the hereafter is more lasting; following Isaiah's grass-withers image in the preceding verse, this verse names God's word as what stands forever, a distinct enduring counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fccd2b6e780f364d1dbd", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "word standing while grass fades", "note": "The commentary moves from the pasture’s passing life to what is better and more enduring; Isaiah counters fading grass with the word of God that stands forever."}
{"connection_id": "BC-19130cfa01dbbdda6cb4", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water and spirit poured on dry ground", "note": "The commentary calls rain the source of earthly life and supplies it as the unstated cause of pasture; Isaiah joins water poured on thirsty ground to God's spirit poured on descendants, giving the rain-and-life image a restoration meaning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74805feaf6b7419f5078", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "water on thirsty ground, life-giving outpouring", "note": "The commentary calls rain ḥayāʾ because it gives life to earth; Isaiah pairs water on thirsty ground and streams on dry land with God’s life-giving outpouring on descendants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71f8efcf28f0aed8d23e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "offspring spring up like grass", "note": "The commentary describes pasture emerging from earth; Isaiah says restored descendants spring up like grass and willows by streams, extending plant growth into a picture of a people renewed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6e0a71bf9a8e1c7f2078", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "grass among waters", "note": "The commentary’s rain leaves green growth on the ground; the next verse says the recipients will spring up among grass like willows beside watercourses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5bda13a23cfaaa6fee8f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.5", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "watered growth beside inscription of the divine name", "note": "The commentary’s alternate derivation of “name” from a mark makes first rain stamp the earth with vegetation; after Isaiah’s water-and-grass image, this verse has people write the Lord’s name as an identifying inscription."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5222b5e5e07c6dbc19df", "ref": "WLC:Isa.45.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain from heaven, salvation sprouts", "note": "The commentary traces a vertical movement from the Most High through rain to pasture; Isaiah calls for the heavens to pour down and the earth to open so salvation and righteousness sprout, giving the plant image a moral-restoration sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c73f2fa2a8af75f7338e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.51.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "perishing heaven and earth, everlasting salvation", "note": "The commentary begins with sky and ends by weighing the passing world against what endures; Isaiah says heaven vanishes and earth wears out, while God’s salvation is forever."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c9e9b7795c9330d11b72", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain and divine word", "note": "The commentary traces a line from the Most High through rain to pasture; Isaiah says rain descends from heaven, waters earth, and makes it sprout, then compares that efficacy to God's word."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa36c8ae3923ca8bcf42", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "rain from heaven makes earth sprout", "note": "The commentary’s sky-to-ground chain makes rain bring out vegetation; Isaiah’s rain and snow descend from heaven, water the earth, and make it bring forth and sprout before the word comparison begins."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06fd7d0225cd0ebf3841", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "well-watered garden and unfailing spring", "note": "The commentary contrasts ordinary grass that dries with plants that escape summer death; Isaiah promises a well-watered garden and a spring whose water does not fail."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f4de74439b78c3bc07e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.66.24", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "unquenched fire and undying worm", "note": "The commentary’s great fire holds its inhabitant in neither ordinary death nor life; Isaiah’s final vision likewise depicts rebels under fire that is not quenched and a worm that does not die."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d93722d0de935a7fb19", "ref": "WLC:Jer.10.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "clouds, lightning, rain, wind", "note": "The commentary’s cloud first flashes and then rains under divine agency; Jeremiah’s storm formula raises clouds, makes lightning with rain, and sends out wind, supplying the same linked elements."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ecddef55a8871de23a0d", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree remains green in heat", "note": "The commentary contrasts the pasture's decay with a source-labeled plant family whose plants do not dry in summer; Jeremiah's water-fed tree has green leaves and does not stop bearing fruit when heat comes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71544dcb328947c50662", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "green leaf and fruit in drought", "note": "The commentary contrasts the pasture’s summer death with a named plant that does not dry; Jeremiah’s water-rooted tree stays green in heat and does not cease bearing fruit in drought."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fa4d698615579ebec266", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "soul like a watered garden, no longer languishing", "note": "The commentary opposes dried pasture to an undying green state; Jeremiah describes restored Israel’s life as a watered garden that will languish no more."}
{"connection_id": "BC-53ceeb610c78b103785d", "ref": "WLC:Job.12.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "deep darkness brought to light", "note": "The commentary connects what is hidden with a faint light in cloud; Job says God exposes deep things from darkness and brings deep gloom into light."}
{"connection_id": "BC-495f7c11c0711342813c", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "man dying unlike the reviving tree", "note": "The commentary uses the pasture’s life and death to frame the human neither-living-nor-dying state; Job sharply distinguishes the tree’s renewal from a human who dies and is no longer found."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84bea94c969c51bc7c55", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water revives a tree", "note": "The commentary's plant begins in barren earth and receives life through rain; Job says a cut tree can bud again at the scent of water, adding a specific image of renewed growth after apparent loss."}

