item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-a1030e2198dd32fb51de", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "old stump budding at the scent of water", "note": "The commentary calls rain the earth’s life and hears fresh, trembling vegetation in the life-root; Job says even an old stump buds at the scent of water and produces branches again."}
{"connection_id": "BC-635705ac20dd0291c78b", "ref": "WLC:Job.36.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "drops drawn up and rain distilled from mist", "note": "The commentary fills the unspoken interval from cloud to rain; Job describes the water cycle as God drawing up drops and distilling rain from mist."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c440937234d80c21811", "ref": "WLC:Job.37.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "moisture-laden cloud and lightning cloud", "note": "The commentary singles out weak lightning at the cloud’s edges before rain; Job describes God loading the cloud with moisture and scattering the cloud of his light, keeping those two features together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3539904ecc67eb24850d", "ref": "WLC:Job.37.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud lightning and knowledge", "note": "The commentary links 87:7's hiddenness to weak lightning at cloud edges; Job asks whether a human knows how God commands the clouds and flashes lightning, joining storm signs to limits on human knowledge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2cb0cf0f9d7bb65bc054", "ref": "WLC:Job.37.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "south wind brings heat", "note": "The commentary says the south wind sustains a cloud and links its Arabic root family to 87:11; Job describes the south wind making garments hot, adding a distinct weather effect for the same named wind."}
{"connection_id": "BC-822c2e1bb9e77a5630d5", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.25", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "path for rain torrent and lightning", "note": "The commentary supplies an unspoken rain between pasture’s emergence and decay and notices lightning at the cloud’s edge; Job asks who cut a channel for the rain-torrent and a path for the thunderbolt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fccb5a6f2fd30db0a1ab", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain on desolate ground", "note": "The commentary says God brings pasture out without naming water; Job describes rain reaching desolate land and causing tender grass to sprout, specifying the same life-giving result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34b2c65695e2af707514", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.27", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "rain satisfying waste, grass sprouting", "note": "The commentary calls rain the life of earth and the cause of the pasture’s emergence; Job says rain satisfies desolate ground and causes the grass to sprout."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b3c9aefb22a7458314c", "ref": "WLC:Job.8.12", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "still-green plant withering before others", "note": "The commentary makes aḥwā hold lush green darkness beside the blackened end of vegetation; Job’s plant is still green and uncut when it withers before other plants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ebeacf1a380bad0a60e", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wilderness pastures sprouting", "note": "The commentary tracks the pasture from first green emergence onward; Joel tells the animals not to fear because the wilderness pastures have sprouted and the trees bear fruit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7875649007f310f3f253", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.23", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "former and latter rain", "note": "The commentary identifies the first year's rain as the wasmi because it marks earth with growth; Joel's restoration oracle promises former and latter rains, placing seasonal rainfall within a renewed agricultural cycle."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d13b6740180ccb2104e7", "ref": "WLC:Joel.2.23", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "former and latter rain", "note": "The commentary focuses on the first rain’s visible mark on the earth; Joel summons Zion to rejoice because God sends the seasonal former and latter rains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6634d54f7e63873bc21c", "ref": "WLC:Jonah.4.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "plant struck and withered", "note": "The commentary stresses that the same creator who brings out pasture turns it into a later state; Jonah’s God appoints a worm to strike the sheltering plant, and it withers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8e721a0c329fc096af5", "ref": "WLC:Lev.26.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "rains in season, land yield, tree fruit", "note": "The commentary makes seasonal rain the cause of pasture’s first appearance; the covenant blessing promises rains in their season, land produce, and fruit from field trees."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1fb86c4503024bc882f2", "ref": "WLC:Num.9.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cloud covering, fire-like appearance at night", "note": "The commentary associates cloud duration with night-watched lightning; at the tabernacle the cloud covers the dwelling and appears as fire from evening until morning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8c757aa44bc9aede049", "ref": "WLC:Num.9.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cloud remaining many days", "note": "The commentary’s Arabic branch hears a cloud that stays rather than passing on; Numbers specifies that when the cloud remained many days over the dwelling, Israel remained camped beneath it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-291404a700a82d03658b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree beside water", "note": "The commentary sets pasture that dries against source-labeled plants said not to dry in summer; Psalm 1's person rooted beside streams remains fruitful with an unwithered leaf, an ethical image of sustained greenness."}
