{"connection_id": "BC-1fb86c4503024bc882f2", "ref": "WLC:Num.9.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cloud covering, fire-like appearance at night", "note": "The commentary associates cloud duration with night-watched lightning; at the tabernacle the cloud covers the dwelling and appears as fire from evening until morning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8c757aa44bc9aede049", "ref": "WLC:Num.9.22", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cloud remaining many days", "note": "The commentary’s Arabic branch hears a cloud that stays rather than passing on; Numbers specifies that when the cloud remained many days over the dwelling, Israel remained camped beneath it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-291404a700a82d03658b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree beside water", "note": "The commentary sets pasture that dries against source-labeled plants said not to dry in summer; Psalm 1's person rooted beside streams remains fruitful with an unwithered leaf, an ethical image of sustained greenness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-65cd1764e9c6d07b1503", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "watered tree whose leaf does not wither", "note": "The commentary sets the ordinary pasture’s drying fate against plants that do not perish in summer; the righteous person is a water-planted tree whose leaf does not wither."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dda69088a9d51122569c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chaff driven away", "note": "The commentary explains غثاء as dried vegetation gathered and carried off by a flood; Psalm 1 likens the wicked to chaff driven away by wind, applying field residue to human moral fate while changing the force that carries it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ded2283cac478171c159", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wicked like wind-driven chaff", "note": "The commentary calls the pasture’s end dry, tasteless debris moved by flood; the psalm turns the contrasting human fate into chaff that wind drives away."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f1dad232489d36b275de", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human life disappears like grass", "note": "The commentary traces grass from green growth to dry residue; this verse says wind passes over the field flower and its place is gone, applying plant disappearance directly to mortal life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-19cde894bc62b1980106", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "human days like grass", "note": "The commentary later lets the plant’s fate interpret human mortality; this psalm explicitly compares human days to grass and a field flower."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce213b48976f9067ca59", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.17", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wind passing and the grass gone", "note": "The commentary’s dead grass is moved away by the elements; the psalm says wind passes over the flower and it is gone, so even its place no longer knows it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b1fc4cb34f984568f6b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.18", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "love endures beyond mortal life", "note": "The commentary joins the plant's disappearance to the claim that the hereafter lasts; after the preceding grass-and-wind image, this verse says God's steadfast love lasts from everlasting to everlasting, supplying a different enduring counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-557c3a28e7aa3ece0096", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass for livestock", "note": "The commentary says المرعى carries grass, grazing, and pasture-place together; this verse says God makes grass grow for cattle and plants for human use, setting pasture in its provision-for-creatures context."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-078e7a7585495c428b4d", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass made to grow for cattle", "note": "The commentary calls rain the earth’s life and pasture’s source; this creation psalm says God causes grass to grow for cattle, keeping the animal-feeding end of the image visible."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9a2f1491382e44865c1d", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.29", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "breath withdrawn, creatures return to dust", "note": "The commentary describes vegetation's passage between life and death; this verse says creatures die and return to dust when God withdraws their breath, framing death as the withdrawal of life-giving power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1925520fc010471e66f4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.29", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "hidden face, death, and return to dust", "note": "The commentary pairs S87:7’s hiddenness with S87:13’s death and life; this psalm says that when God hides his face creatures are dismayed, lose breath, die, and return to dust."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a953ad8e079991125cc9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.30", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earth renewed by God's breath", "note": "The commentary says the Creator brings pasture out and links rain-fed growth with life; this verse says God sends his spirit, creates living things, and renews the face of the ground, joining creaturely life to renewed earth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-44e849167a287728effe", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.30", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sent breath creating and renewing the ground", "note": "The commentary makes earth’s rain-borne life a bridge to restoration; the answering verse has God send breath, create living beings, and renew the face of the ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-168fd5e1cf5903b4bde3", "ref": "WLC:Ps.105.39", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud as covering, fire by night", "note": "The commentary explains the sky as what rises over and shades, then describes night lightning around clouds; this Exodus retelling says God spread a cloud as covering and gave fire for night light, joining shade and illumination in one image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6f19aa79cc124e723225", "ref": "WLC:Ps.105.39", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "cloud for covering, fire for night light", "note": "The commentary defines sky as what stands overhead and shades, then hears faint night lightning at the cloud’s edge; this psalm says God spread a cloud for covering and fire to give light by night."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6854d63d4b42ea855c68", "ref": "WLC:Ps.129.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "roof grass withers before harvest", "note": "The commentary describes grass emerging in patches and later drying into debris; this curse pictures grass on a roof withering before it can be harvested, adding failed growth rather than a full crop cycle."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e780c993d7d859f7ab8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.135.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "lightning for rain, wind from storehouses", "note": "The commentary’s weak edge-lightning and wind-driven cloud are not separate images here: the psalm has God make lightning for rain and bring wind from storehouses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e590de5bce13de8c9bb", "ref": "WLC:Ps.147.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cloud, rain, grass", "note": "The commentary supplies an unnamed rain between 87:4's pasture and 87:5's residue; this verse says God covers heaven with clouds, prepares rain for earth, and makes grass grow on hills, giving that whole vertical sequence in one scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1dcd6e368377f7a4798", "ref": "WLC:Ps.147.8", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sky-covering cloud, rain, mountain grass", "note": "The commentary’s vertical column runs from an overhead covering through cloud and rain to grass; this psalm says God covers the sky with clouds, prepares rain for earth, and makes grass grow on the mountains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0390fe3da0222afc61d6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.148.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "name and exaltation", "note": "The commentary glosses 87:1's اسم as “name”; the Hebrew cognate שֵׁם appears in a call to praise God's name, which the verse says is exalted above earth and heaven, closely joining name-praise with height."}
{"connection_id": "BC-925bbf44f9ae5a7fdc0a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.148.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "praise of the exalted divine name", "note": "The commentary begins “glorify the name of your Most High Lord”; the psalm calls all creation to praise YHWH’s name because that name alone is exalted."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ac0793023a0f6097287", "ref": "WLC:Ps.23.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "green pasture beside water", "note": "The commentary says المرعى includes grass, grazing, and the place of grazing; the psalm's shepherd leads a speaker to green pastures and still waters, giving the term a concrete setting of rest and nourishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a954748b5877a9fb4b50", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.10", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God visiting and richly watering earth", "note": "The commentary calls rain the life of earth that produces pasture; this psalm says God visits the earth, waters it, and greatly enriches it in preparation for grain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-52dceebc1c15f42d78d5", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "showers soften furrows", "note": "The commentary says first growth appears unevenly, in some places but not others; this verse describes God watering furrows, softening clods with showers, and blessing growth, adding the prepared-soil detail behind that patchy emergence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-edfe23461bb0573187d5", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.11", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "furrows softened by showers and growth blessed", "note": "The commentary’s first rain leaves a visible green mark in the soil; the psalm describes water in furrows, showers softening ridges, and God blessing the earth’s growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c885e6e19418dcf7bc2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.65.13", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wilderness pastures overflowing", "note": "The commentary treats marʿā as grass and pasture-place, not an abstract crop; after the rain imagery this psalm says the wilderness pastures overflow with abundance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d847d2c7bc059777f0e6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.72.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain on mown grass", "note": "The commentary's pasture is a plant-life scene; this royal prayer compares the king's life-giving justice to rain on mown grass and showers on earth, adding a political use for the same agricultural image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-012339fadd0372703e2b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flood-swept grass", "note": "The commentary compresses a plant's life into a brief cycle and explains غثاء as vegetation gathered and carried by a flood; Moses' psalm says human life is swept away like a flood and compares people to grass, joining mortality to both images."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9cc7f95d11ad928048e5", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.6", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "morning grass changing away", "note": "The commentary compresses the plant’s whole lifetime from emergence to decay; this psalm compares human generations to grass that changes in the morning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-438da9103bafcd6ed1ab", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass flourishes, then withers", "note": "The commentary follows pasture from emergence to dry residue; in a psalm attributed to Moses, grass flourishes in the morning and withers by evening, giving the same fast plant cycle a human-mortality setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4bd51f0a856073207abe", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.7", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "grass flourishing then withering by evening", "note": "The commentary’s green pasture becomes dried residue; the next line says the grass flourishes and changes in the morning but fades and withers in the evening."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d707d3c452918f404be2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.91.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Most High, shelter, and shadow", "note": "The commentary calls the Lord “Most High” and defines the sky by its overarching shade; this psalm places the one dwelling in the shelter of the Most High in the shadow of the Almighty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ede66f7c52a93becbea1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.92.15", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "green growth in old age", "note": "The commentary contrasts the pasture's short life with plants said not to dry in summer; this verse says the righteous still bear fruit in old age and remain green, extending that counter-image into a portrait of enduring life."}

