{"connection_id": "BC-b9ed4552cdf692edbe65", "ref": "WLC:Ps.97.2", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "clouds around God's throne", "note": "The commentary connects the Most High with clouds that nurture plants; this theophany surrounds God with clouds and thick darkness while grounding his throne in justice, making cloud a sign of divine presence as well as weather."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3fe0264f51c9d5b15c47", "ref": "WLC:Ps.97.4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lightning seen by the earth", "note": "The commentary describes weak lightning at cloud edges and says God knows what is hidden; this verse says God's lightning lights the world and the earth sees and trembles, pairing a visible storm sign with divine sovereignty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-35b67e7cdf9f42d1e772", "ref": "WLC:Ps.97.9", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "YHWH Most High over all earth", "note": "The commentary’s opening title is “the Most High”; this psalm explicitly calls YHWH Most High over all the earth, placing exalted lordship over the created field and sky."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eec612a04965137816d6", "ref": "WLC:Song.4.16", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "south wind blowing on a garden", "note": "The commentary makes the south wind a secondary branch of the word for turning aside and lets it drive the cloud; the Song directly summons the south wind to blow upon a garden so its scents flow."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d08422177575f7379176", "ref": "WLC:Zech.10.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain, storm clouds, vegetation", "note": "The commentary reconstructs rain as the missing cause of pasture; Zechariah joins a request for seasonal rain to storm clouds, showers, and plants in the field, making the same weather-to-growth link explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a6fcedefd7f0170c62b", "ref": "WLC:Zech.10.1", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "storm-cloud, rain, grass in each field", "note": "The commentary traces cloud, lightning, rain, and pasture as one descent; Zechariah tells people to ask YHWH, who makes storm clouds, gives showers, and gives vegetation to each field, making that full sequence explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0418c6f61458ea32741d", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 3:4", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "hope full of immortality", "note": "The commentary contrasts the world’s drying life with what abides; Wisdom says that, though the righteous seem to have died, their hope is full of immortality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b59a247caaf1720290fb", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human life like chaff", "note": "The commentary notes that Qur'anic غثاء can describe people swept away after a cry; Wisdom has the wicked confess that their life and hope vanish like wind-driven chaff, carrying the plant-debris image into judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-00f2acdf60dd88002689", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:14", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "ungodly hope swept like light chaff or froth", "note": "The commentary’s ghuthā is dry debris carried away and can figure people; Wisdom similarly compares the ungodly’s hope to light material driven off by wind and storm, though English renderings vary in the precise plant term."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc11a17c154e662bce22", "ref": "b. Ta'anit 2a", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "rain named with resurrection in rabbinic prayer", "note": "The commentary makes rain the life of earth and uses it as a bridge toward resurrection; this discussion places the mention of rain in the blessing for revival of the dead and argues from that liturgical pairing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccb7b1f2c7aa1f5d3433", "ref": "b. Ta'anit 7a", "target": "sec1", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "day of rain compared with resurrection of the dead", "note": "The commentary’s rain-to-life-to-resurrection chain receives an explicit rabbinic comparison here, where the day of rain is measured against the resurrection of the dead."}

